\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

Step-independent training collapses while episode-level training succeeds; a factorial design separates the two mechanisms; and window size $h$ modulates severity.

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate on ALFWorld~\cite{shridhar2021alfworld}, a text-based household environment with six task types requiring sequential reasoning over up to 50 steps. The environment provides ternary reward: $R \in \{r_-, 0, r_+\}$, where $r_- = -0.1$ per invalid action, $0$ for valid but unsuccessful actions, and $r_+$ for task success. Our theoretical analysis (Section~\ref{sec:theory}) assumes the simplified binary case ($R \in \{0, r_+\}$), which yields an upper bound on sign misattribution severity; Section~\ref{sec:sign_verification} validates this bound under both reward structures. The base policy is Qwen3-1.7B fine-tuned on expert demonstrations (SFT baseline: 12.5\% in-distribution). At this baseline success rate, Proposition~2 predicts the majority of gradient updates carry incorrect sign. All conditions use RAGEN~\cite{ragen2025} (v0.1, commit \texttt{3a6b48b})\footnote{Our implementation extends RAGEN v0.1 with episode-level training support. Code available upon acceptance.} with GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} ($K{=}8$; full hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{app:hyperparameters}). We report success rate on a 134-task OOD set (primary) and 32-task in-distribution set (secondary). Seed counts are noted per condition.

\subsection{Factorial Mechanism Separation}
\label{sec:factorial_experiments}

We implement the factorial design from Section~\ref{sec:factorial_design} with two seeds per condition. Cell~C (full observation, step-level advantage) would isolate M2 without M1, but we encountered persistent training instability in this configuration (Appendix~\ref{app:cell_c}). Cell~C's NaN divergence, qualitatively different from Cell~A's gradual collapse, suggests that information loss may have an unintended stabilizing effect on gradient variance, and that the M1--M2 interaction may be non-additive (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).

\subsection{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cell~A collapses in two phases (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}): task success drops to 0\% while action validity initially remains high, then continued sign misattribution destroys even basic formatting. % exp_id: hsweep_h2_seed1, hsweep_h2_seed2
Cell~B eliminates sign misattribution while retaining information loss: training success improves, but OOD generalization is bounded by the information-loss ceiling. Both seeds converge to the same OOD ceiling despite different training trajectories, confirming the bottleneck is architectural rather than statistical.% exp_id: factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed1, factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed2

Cell~D is the only condition that substantially exceeds the SFT baseline, with monotonic OOD improvement across both seeds.% exp_id: exp029_episode_level, exp029_seed3_ood_v2

The A$\to$B comparison isolates M2: eliminating sign misattribution converts catastrophic collapse into bounded learning. The B$\to$D comparison isolates M1: removing the information-loss ceiling enables OOD generalization. Both mechanisms are necessary to explain the full failure pattern. Figure~\ref{fig:hsweep}(a) visualizes these trajectories.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_training_dynamics.pdf}
\caption{(a)~Training dynamics across factorial conditions (metric type indicated in legend). Cell~A collapses within 5 steps; Cell~B shows bounded learning; Cell~D improves monotonically. (b)~Window size $h$ vs.\ success rate (seed~1). $h{\leq}20$ bars use a 32-task in-distribution eval set; the episode-level bar uses a 134-task OOD set (dotted separator); magnitudes are not directly comparable across eval sets. A credit assignment threshold between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ separates collapse from recovery. Hatched bars indicate collapsed conditions.}
\label{fig:hsweep}
\end{figure*}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{1.7B results on ALFWorld (best checkpoint per seed, evaluated on held-out test sets). In-Dist: 32-task set; OOD: 134-task set. Cell~A reports best seed (2 seeds); Cell~B reports best checkpoint at step~5 (2 seeds). Cell~D reports mean of 2 seeds (In-Dist range: 28.1--40.6; OOD range: 10.4--23.9); Cell~D results are consistent with the 11-seed evaluation (Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability}), where mean OOD$=$17.2\% with 36\% generalization rate. Dense reward and REINFORCE++: 1 seed each.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Mechanisms} & \textbf{In-Dist (\%)} & \textbf{OOD (\%)} \\
\midrule
SFT baseline & --- & 12.50 & 6.72 \\
Cell A ($h{=}2$, step) & M1+M2 & 0 & 0 \\
Cell B ($h{=}2$, episode) & M1 only & 18.75 & 5.97 \\
Cell D (full, episode) & neither & \textbf{34.38} & \textbf{17.16} \\
\midrule
Dense reward & M1+M2 & 0 & 0 \\
REINFORCE++ & neither & 12.50 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
% exp_id: hsweep_h2_seed1, factorial_h2_episodeadv_seed1, exp029_episode_level, exp029_seed3_v2, exp013f, gae_baseline_seed1
\end{table}

\paragraph{Additional conditions.} Dense reward, REINFORCE++, and discount-factor sweep experiments all confirm that sign misattribution, not reward sparsity or discounting, drives the failure (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\paragraph{Window-size sweep.} Varying window size reveals a credit assignment threshold between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ that separates collapse from recovery (Figure~\ref{fig:hsweep}b; seed~1). Below this threshold, all conditions collapse to 0\%; above it, success rate increases monotonically with window size. This threshold is observed in a single seed; multi-seed verification is needed to confirm its robustness.% exp_id: h_sweep_series, exp029_seed1
\label{sec:results_hsweep}

\subsection{Sign Misattribution Verification}
\label{sec:sign_verification}

We distinguish three metrics that each capture a different aspect of credit assignment quality:

\textit{(1) Sign-episode correlation}: whether advantage sign tracks episode outcome (success/failure). Under pure binary reward (penalty${=}0$), this correlation is 100\% ($n{=}157$ windows; Figure~\ref{fig:sign_misattribution}), directly confirming M2: advantage sign is entirely determined by episode outcome, not local action quality. This is by construction, since all windows in a successful episode share the same positive return under binary reward.% exp_id: binary_credit_agreement_iter0

\textit{(2) Credit agreement rate}: whether advantage sign matches local action quality, operationalized through action validity as a proxy for local correctness (Definition~1). Under step-independent training with penalty reward ($h{=}2$, $n{=}3{,}000$ windows), only 52.07\% of windows with valid actions receive positive advantage, marginally above random chance ($p{=}0.025$ vs.\ 50\%; $+$2.07pp effect size). This near-random credit agreement shows that advantage sign carries almost no information about local action quality. Under pure binary reward, credit agreement drops further to 28\% ($n{=}271$), below random chance---advantage sign systematically punishes valid actions in failed episodes. Penalty reward partially breaks sign-episode correlation by introducing within-group variance, but the resulting signal remains too weak for meaningful credit assignment. This operationalization is conservative: successful episodes may contain suboptimal windows, making our validity label an upper bound on true local correctness. Even under this generous criterion, credit agreement remains near-random, strengthening the diagnosis.% exp_id: 7b_cell_a_h2

\textit{(3) $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update})$}: the theoretical upper bound from Proposition~2 ($\leq$19.1\% at $p{=}12.5\%$, $K{=}8$). This bounds the fraction of non-zero gradient updates with correct sign, a different quantity from the credit agreement rate, which conditions on action validity rather than gradient participation. The two metrics are complementary: Proposition~2 bounds worst-case gradient quality; credit agreement measures empirical signal strength.

Combined with $\gamma{=}1.0$ collapse (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}), these measurements are consistent with sign misattribution as the primary contributor to training failure.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_sign_misattribution.pdf}
\caption{Sign-episode correlation (binary reward, penalty${=}0$) vs.\ credit agreement rate (penalty reward). Under binary reward, advantage sign perfectly tracks episode outcome (100\%, $n{=}157$), confirming M2. Under penalty reward, credit agreement with local action quality drops to 52.1\% ($n{=}3{,}000$), near random chance (dashed line). These are distinct metrics: the former measures episode-level sign correlation, the latter measures local credit assignment quality.}
\label{fig:sign_misattribution}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Episode-Level Training: Seed Variability}
\label{sec:seed_variability}

Episode-level GRPO addresses the dual-mechanism failure, but training outcomes remain seed-dependent. We systematically evaluate $N{=}11$ random seeds under identical hyperparameters, revealing a $2{\times}2$ structure: \textit{training stability} (collapsed vs.\ non-collapsed) $\times$ \textit{OOD generalization} (stagnant vs.\ generalized).

We define \textbf{collapsed} seeds as those where in-distribution success declines $>$50\% from peak and remains below baseline for $\geq$3 consecutive steps. \textbf{Non-collapsed} seeds are the complement. Among non-collapsed seeds, we further distinguish \textbf{generalized} (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT on the 134-task OOD set) from \textbf{stagnant} (not significant vs.\ SFT). Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability} reports results for each seed at its best-performing checkpoint.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Episode-level GRPO across 11 seeds (134 OOD tasks, $\tau{=}0.4$, best checkpoint per seed). Category: C=Collapsed, S=Non-collapsed (stagnant), G=Generalized (Fisher exact $p{<}0.01$ vs.\ SFT). Wilson 95\% CI. SFT baseline: 6.72\% (9/134). Seed2 achieves $p{=}0.03$ (Fisher exact, two-sided), falling outside the conservative $p{<}0.01$ threshold, and is classified as Stagnant. Step-5 results for all seeds in Appendix~\ref{app:step5_unified}.}
\label{tab:seed_variability}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
